Spoilers ahead:
- A bandit is gonna one-hit you with a thrown axe just as you enter the map with your newly created character;
- You're gonna starve to death after equipping an autocursing amulet of hunger and not being able to destroy or remove it;
- You're gonna drown in an underground river, even though you're actually a reasonable swimmer;
- A gelatinous cube is gonna paralyse you while some flimsy goblin beats the♥♥♥♥♥♥out of you;
- You're gonna get trapped by a horde of monsters you can't possibly beat because you just HAD to grab that piece of loot;
- A vampire is gonna drain your strenght until you can't move without emptying your inventory, and even if you somehow survive, it's permanent until you train it again;
- The RNG is gonna destroy your most valuable possessions when you get hit by item-destroying effects. Repeatedly;
- You're gonna accidentaly kill a cat and know you're ♥♥♥♥ed;
- A gorgon is gonna trap you in a room and instakill you with it's petrifying breath;
- You're gonna think that eating that strange monster's corpse is not gonna do you any harm, and then you're gonna regret it, possibly a lot;
- You're gonna try and use a wand of ball lighting to rid yourself of dangerous enemies surrounding you and the bolt is gonna bounce on the walls and hit you multiple times, until you lost hitpoints enough to die 3 times over.
- After you die, you're gonna think of all the things you could have done to save yourself, and if it seems like it was at all avoidable, you're gonna feel like an idiot.
And then you're gonna learn from that and come back for more.
That's what makes this game great.
Couple it with an open-world, roleplaying and epic feel, a great variety of races and classes, infinite possibilities on how to approach and deal with stuff, a simple yet cool plot, and you got yourself a game you should really be playing right now.
PS: I might not have that many hours on record, but I have played this game for quite some time before it was on Steam, too.

